/**
 * Computed signatures of the `tiles` module (C++ operators, modules/tiles).
 *
 * Of the nine operators the module exports, `available` and `device` have fixed signatures and
 * are declared in stdlib/tiles.cml. The seven entries here take their signature from an argument
 * that a declaration cannot name:
 *   - `upload` wraps every leaf of its argument in the device handle `Device<...>`, `download`
 *     strips that handle from every leaf;
 *   - `run_preferred_resident` takes the function's parameter list (or the tuple of it) as the
 *     ordinary parameter and answers the function's result type, wrapped in `Device<...>`;
 *   - `source`, `inspect` and `benchmark` answer text, but their ordinary parameter is still the
 *     function's parameter list;
 *   - `regions` takes any function value as its with-parameter.
 * The `DynamicFuncTypeResolver` of modules/tiles is the one source of these signatures, and the
 * `tiles` module keeps `Device<...>` as its own type (exported by C++, named here for the
 * reference text). Each entry below writes one instance of its rule.
 *
 * For reference only: these declarations are not compiled. They are checked against the operators
 * the module registers, and docs/stdlib-reference.md is generated from them.
 */

/// Uploads every leaf of the value — a float or an f32 tensor, alone or inside a tuple or a
/// struct — and returns the same tree with each leaf wrapped in `Device<...>`. A leaf of any other
/// type is refused when the call is compiled.
/// @effect transfers to the device
/// @example upload(host)    // a Device<Tensor<f32>>
export func upload(value: Tensor<f32>): Device<Tensor<f32>>

/// Downloads every device leaf of the value and returns the host value it came from. A tree with a
/// leaf that is not a device value is refused when the call is compiled.
/// @effect transfers from the device
/// @example download(resident)    // the tensor resident holds
export func download(value: Device<Tensor<f32>>): Tensor<f32>

/// Runs the function through the device path the pipeline prepared for it — the device when a
/// program can be made for it, the host otherwise — and answers the resident form of its result.
/// This is the operator `tiles::compile` inserts for an outlined region; a `#gpu` scope asks for
/// the device explicitly, and an operator the device cannot run is a compile error there.
/// @effect transfers to the device and runs device code
/// @example run_preferred_resident<(a: float, b: float): float => a + b>((2.0, 3.0))    // a Device<float>
with <fn: (a: float, b: float) => float>
export func run_preferred_resident(inputs: (float, float)): Device<float>

/// The CUDA source the function compiles to, without running it.
/// @effect compiles device code
/// @example println(source<(a: float, b: float): float => a + b>((2.0, 3.0)))
with <fn: (a: float, b: float) => float>
export func source(inputs: (float, float)): string

/// A report on the function's device plan, without running it: the kernel, its launch shape and
/// the values it would keep on the device.
/// @effect compiles device code
/// @example println(inspect<(a: float, b: float): float => a + b>((2.0, 3.0)))
with <fn: (a: float, b: float) => float>
export func inspect(inputs: (float, float)): string

/// Times the function on the device, and on the host for comparison, and returns the report as
/// JSON text.
/// @effect transfers both ways and runs device code
/// @example println(benchmark<(a: float, b: float): float => a + b>((2.0, 3.0)))
with <fn: (a: float, b: float) => float>
export func benchmark(inputs: (float, float)): string

/// The regions the compiler would take for the whole program, as text: which nodes would run on
/// the device and why the others would not.
/// @effect compiles device code
/// @example println(regions<(a: float, b: float): float => a + b>())
with <fn: (a: float, b: float) => float>
export func regions(): string